---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_LAUNCH_DATE } from '../lib/seo';
import { openDatasets, findDataset } from '../lib/open-data';

// The sourcing standard below is stated with MEASURED figures, not asserted
// ones: the same build-time provenance measurement /data/ publishes drives the
// numbers here, so this page can never claim a standard the data does not meet.
const datasets = openDatasets();
const fines = findDataset('fine-schedule', datasets);
const states = findDataset('state-records', datasets);
const schemes = findDataset('discount-schemes', datasets);
const rto = findDataset('rto-codes', datasets);
---
<Base
  title="Editorial Policy"
  description="How TrafficChallan sources facts, corrects errors, and keeps fine and procedure pages up to date — including our AI-assistance disclosure."
  path="/editorial-policy/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Editorial policy', path: '/editorial-policy/' }]}
  dateModified={SITE_LAUNCH_DATE}
>
  <h1>Editorial policy</h1>

  <p>
    This page explains how we decide what to publish, where our facts come from, how we fix mistakes,
    and how content on this site gets written in the first place.
  </p>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <p>
    Fine amounts, Motor Vehicles Act sections, portal URLs, payment methods and procedural steps are
    sourced to an official government document: parivahan.gov.in, a state police or transport department
    portal, India Code, or a gazette notification. Forum posts, news reports and aggregator sites are never
    sources for any of those, even when we link to them for context. Where a state has notified its own
    fine amount that differs from the national schedule, we cite that state notification specifically
    rather than assuming the national figure applies. Both datasets meet that standard in full:
    {fines.provenance.official} of {fines.provenance.citations} citations across the fine schedule and
    {' '}{states.provenance.official} of {states.provenance.citations} across the state records are
    official — {fines.provenance.officialPct}% and {states.provenance.officialPct}%, counted at build time
    from the records themselves.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two of our datasets do not meet that standard, and we would rather say so here than let you find it
    yourself. The <a href="/rto-codes/">RTO office directory</a> is
    {' '}{rto.provenance.officialPct}% officially sourced ({rto.provenance.official} of
    {' '}{rto.provenance.citations} citations); {rto.provenance.filesWithoutOfficial.length} of its
    {' '}{rto.provenance.files} state files rest entirely on independent commercial directories —
    insurance, used-car and vehicle-information sites — because we could not find a fetchable official
    code list for most states. The
    {' '}<a href="/challan-discount/">discount and amnesty scheme tracker</a> is
    {' '}{schemes.provenance.officialPct}% official: a scheme we record as a rumour or a proposal is by
    definition a claim that no government order confirms, so press reporting and fact-checks are the only
    evidence there is — and our schema refuses to store a discount percentage on those records at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the rule is per record, not per site: every record names the URLs it was actually checked against,
    and the measured official share of each dataset is published — and re-measured on every build — at
    {' '}<a href="/data/#provenance">the open-data page</a>. Where a page rests on something other than an
    official document, the page says so on its face.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If you find a wrong fine amount, an outdated section reference, a dead portal link, or any other
    factual error, email <a href="mailto:contact@trafficchallan.com">contact@trafficchallan.com</a>. We
    aim to investigate and correct verified errors within 7 days. If the underlying source has genuinely
    changed, we update the page and its "last verified" date at the same time — we don't silently edit
    facts without moving that date.
  </p>

  <h2>Update policy</h2>
  <p>
    Fine amounts and procedures change when governments issue new notifications, so pages don't stay
    accurate on their own. Every page carries a "last verified" date. We re-check every page against its
    sources on a rolling 90-day cycle, and our build process is set up to flag any page whose facts
    haven't been re-verified inside that window, so stale information doesn't sit published indefinitely.
    When a fact changes, the page and its date both change together — the date is a claim about the
    content next to it, not a general timestamp.
  </p>

  <h2>AI assistance</h2>
  <p>
    Content on this site is researched and drafted with AI assistance, then checked against the sourcing
    standards above before publication. We're saying this plainly rather than hiding it: AI helps us pull
    together and phrase information faster, but it does not get to invent facts — every number and rule
    still has to trace back to the sources listed on the record, held to the standard set out above, and
    that discipline doesn't change based on how a page was drafted.
  </p>
</Base>
